※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4568

※ A lifetime name and `from` answer the same question, one by naming something
※ the signature carries in several places and one by naming a parameter
※ outright.  Written together they would either say the same thing twice or
※ disagree, and neither is worth reading.
※
※ `from b` is the shorter way where there is one parameter to name, and `⧖x` is
※ the general one; which of them a signature uses is its own business, and
※ using both is not a third thing to say.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    0u6

fn f(b: &mut ⧖x u32) → &mut ⧖x u32 from b:
    b
